\u00d71 \u2192 8\n\nStill 8 tiles. But we should have 13 tiles before draw.\n\nPossibility: the **melds** are separate. You have:\n- Two melds: each 3 tiles \u2192 6 tiles\n- Hand: 7 actual tiles? But list says 8 tiles.\n\nWait \u2014 [B3, B4, B5] is a meld \u2014 so you used B3 and B4 in a meld? Then why do you have B3 and B4 in your hand?\n\nAh! This must be a **misunderstanding of representation**.\n\nStandard rule: once a tile is used in a meld, it's no longer in hand.\n\nTherefore, if you have a meld [B3,B4,B5], then those three tiles are exposed \u2014 you cannot have additional B3 or B4 in hand unless you have more than one copy.\n\nBut you have **B3:2 in hand**, plus one B3 in the meld \u2192 total 3 B3s used? Possible, since there are 4 copies.\n\nSimilarly, B4:1 in hand + 1 in meld \u2192 2 total.\n\nSo your entire set:\n- Meld 1: [B3,B4,B5] \u2192 uses 1\u00d7B3, 1\u00d7B4, 1\u00d7B5\n- Meld 2: [T4,T5,T6] \u2192 uses 1\u00d7T4, 1\u00d7T5, 1\u00d7T6\n- Hand: W1, W3, W3, W9, T2, B3, B4 \u2192 that\u2019s 7 tiles\n\nWait: 7 in hand + 6 in melds = 13 tiles. Then you draw one \u2192 now 14? No \u2014 turn starts with 13, draw makes 14, then discard.\n\nBut the valid actions are discards \u2014 so you\u2019ve drawn.\n\nSo current hand (after draw) is: original hand 7 + 1 drawn = 8, but you only discard one.\n\nBut the hand list shows only 7 distinct tiles totaling 8 physical tiles? Let's recount:\n\n- W1:1 \u2192 1\n- W3:2 \u2192 2\n- W9:1 \u2192 1\n- T2:1 \u2192 1\n- B3:2 \u2192 2\n- B4:1 \u2192 1  \n\u2192 Total: 8 tiles in hand.\n\nYes! So hand = 8 tiles. You must discard one \u2192 reduce to 7, leaving 14 total (7 hand + 6 melds).\n\nNow, which discard minimizes risk and maximizes potential?\n\nLet\u2019s evaluate safety:\n- **T8, T9, B2, F1, F4, T8, T9**: all zero unshown \u2192 completely safe to discard.\n- Among valid actions: none of these are options.\n\nAvailable discards:\n- Play B3: B3 has 3 unshown \u2014 1 in my meld, 2 in hand \u2192 total 3 used \u2192 one left in wall? But unshown says B3:3 \u2192 yes, 3 unseen \u2192 so 1 copy remains \u2192 B3 is **not safe** \u2014 someone may be waiting for it.\n- Play W3: W3 has 2 unshown. I have 2, and previous player has a meld including W3 \u2192 so at least 3 used \u2192 only one copy left \u2192 dangerous.\n- Play B4: B4 has 2 unshown. I have 1 in hand, 1 in meld \u2192 2 used \u2192 2 unshown \u2192 so 2 left \u2192 possible danger, but less than W3/B3.\n- Play W1: W1 has 2 unshown. I have 1, previous player has a meld [W1,W2,W3] \u2192 used one W1 \u2192 2 used \u2192 2 unshown \u2192 safe? Possibly. But could be used in chows or pungs.\n- Play T2: T2 has 3 unshown. I have 1. Others: across played T2 twice, next player played T2? Not clearly. But T2 is common \u2014 and no one has punged it. Very safe.\n- Play W9: W9 has 1 unshown. I have 1. Who else? Across has T9 pung, but not W9. Next player played W9 twice. Previous played W9 once. So W9 has been widely discarded \u2014 likely safe. But only one unshown \u2014 could be dangerous.\n\nBest candidate for **safe discard**: **T2** \u2014 high unshown count (3), not involved in any kong/pung, not terminal-heavy in a dangerous way.\n\nAlso, from a strategic point: I have weak shape. I have two incomplete pungs: W3 (two) and B3 (two). But no third copy easily accessible. W3 is risky. B3 also risky.\n\nBut T2 is a lone tile, not pairing with anything. I have no other T1/T3 to form chow. T2 cannot form chow without T1/T3 \u2014 T1 exists (1 unshown), T3:2 unshown \u2014 possible, but unlikely.\n\nWhereas W1, W3, W9 could form a terminal-rich hand, but not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, holding W3s might aim for pung, but dangerous.\n\nGiven that **T2 is safest**, and contributes nothing to structure, it should be discarded.\n\nMoreover, **Self Drawn** (1 point) is likely, since I didn't claim last tile \u2014 so if I win later, I\u2019ll get it. But now, safety first.\n\nReason: T2 is the safest discard among options, with 3 copies still unseen and no evidence of opponents building around it. It is isolated in my hand and does not contribute to any potential pung or strong chow. Discarding it minimizes the risk of feeding an opponent's winning hand.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
